%description:

Tcp.sendMssOption:off-peer -- without the MSS option of host1, the peer sends segments of at most 536 bytes.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "send the MSS option"; RFC 9293 MUST-15: without an MSS option
the peer uses the default of 536 bytes (IPv4).

The class off-peer: TcpConnection::applyDefaultSendMss() of the peer limits its SMSS to 536,
and TcpAlgorithmBase::getReceiveMss() of host1 takes 536 as its RMSS. This facet comes from the coverage gap loop: no test reached the code of this part. This test separates
"off" from "on", where the peer sends 1448 bytes.

Host under test: host1, which listens at the port 1000; host2 connects at 0.1 s and sends 10000
bytes at 0.2 s. Observation: level 1, the first data segment of host2 carries at most 536
bytes of data.

%# parameter: Tcp.sendMssOption
%# class: off-peer
%# value: false
%# key: *.host1.tcp.sendMssOption
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpSendMssOptionOffPeer")
        .once(on("host2.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("pk.dataLength > tcp.headerLength")
             .first()
                  .assertPacket("pk.dataLength - tcp.headerLength <= 536B")
             .describe("the first data segment of host2")
             .within(1.0));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpSendMssOptionOffPeer compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host1.tcp.sendMssOption = false    # the parameter under test
*.host1.app[0].typename = "TcpEchoApp"
*.host1.app[0].localPort = 1000
*.host1.app[0].echoFactor = 0
*.host2.app[0].typename = "TcpSessionApp"
*.host2.app[0].connectAddress = "host1"
*.host2.app[0].connectPort = 1000
*.host2.app[0].tOpen = 0.1s
*.host2.app[0].tSend = 0.2s
*.host2.app[0].sendBytes = 10000B
*.host2.app[0].tClose = 1000s
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpSendMssOptionOffPeer: PASS
